templ consulBasicUsage() {
	@docLead() {
		A caller needs one thing from Consul: the healthy instances of a service, by name. These are the lines the consul/service-discovery starter's caller runs, using HashiCorp's official Go client, @docCodeWord("github.com/hashicorp/consul/api")&#46;
	}
	@docH2("connect", "Connecting")
	@docP() {
		Breadboard sets @docCodeWord("CONSUL_HTTP_ADDR") to @docCodeWord("https://consul.bb.internal:8501") and @docCodeWord("CONSUL_CACERT") to the trust bundle. The client reads both, so the code has no address or certificate path in it.
	}
	@docCode("cmd/caller/main.go", consulConnectCode)
	@docP() {
		What actually happens: @docCodeWord("DefaultConfig") starts from @docCodeWord("localhost:8500") over HTTP, then overrides it from the environment. @docCodeWord("NewClient") sees the @docCodeWord("https://") prefix on the address, switches the scheme to HTTPS, and builds an HTTP client that verifies the server's certificate against the CA file. It does not contact Consul yet; every later call is one HTTPS request to the address.
	}
	@docH2("lookup", "Looking up a service")
	@docCode("cmd/caller/main.go", consulLookupCode)
	@docP() {
		The arguments are the service name, a tag to filter on (empty means any), @docCodeWord("passingOnly"), and query options. The call becomes @docCodeWord("GET /v1/health/service/greeter?passing=1")&#46; With @docCodeWord("passing"), the server returns only instances whose checks are all in the passing state, so the filtering happens in Consul, not in your code.
	}
	@docP() {
		Each entry carries the instance's @docCodeWord("Node"), its @docCodeWord("Checks"), and its @docCodeWord("Service"), which holds the ID, name, address, port and tags. An empty list is a normal answer: it means no instance is healthy right now.
	}
	@docH2("calling", "Calling an instance")
	@docCode("cmd/caller/main.go", consulCallCode)
	@docP() {
		Consul is not in the request path. It answered which instances exist; the caller picks one at random and connects to it directly. The address is the name on the greeter's certificate, so the HTTPS call verifies against the trust bundle like any other.
	}
	@docCallout("Asking on every call") {
		The starter looks up the service before each call, every 2 seconds. That keeps the example obvious. A busy service would cache the list and refresh it with a blocking query (see @docLink("/docs/consul/kv", "The key-value store"), which explains blocking queries).
	}
	@docH2("run", "Run it")
	@docTerminal("bb init consul-lab", "bb starter consul/service-discovery", "bb up", "bb logs caller -f")
}

templ consulRegistration() {
	@docLead() {
		Something has to tell Consul that an instance exists and how to check it. Breadboard offers two ways. The recommended one is a sidecar agent that registers the app for it, so the app contains no Consul code. The alternative is for the service to register itself through the Consul API.
	}
	@docFigure("The agent shares the greeter's network namespace, so it has the greeter's address and certificate.") {
		@consulDiagram()
	}
	@docH2("sidecar", "With a sidecar (recommended)")
	@docP() {
		Add @docCodeWord("register") to the app in @docCodeWord("experiment.yaml")&#46; @docCodeWord("port") is the port the app serves TLS on. Optional @docCodeWord("name") sets the registered service name (default: the app's name) and @docCodeWord("check") sets an HTTPS health check path. The app must also have the Consul service in @docCodeWord("uses")&#46;
	}
	@docCode("experiment.yaml", consulRegisterYAML)
	@docP() {
		The greeter itself only serves HTTPS. It does not know Consul exists:
	}
	@docCode("cmd/greeter/main.go", consulGreeterCode)
	@docH2("generated", "What Breadboard generates")
	@docP() {
		For an app with @docCodeWord("register"), Breadboard adds a compose service named @docCodeWord("greeter-agent")&#46; It runs the Consul image as a client agent with @docCodeWord("network_mode: service:greeter"), so it shares the greeter's network namespace: the same address and the same DNS name. It mounts the greeter's certificate and key rather than having its own. Its configuration, written to @docCodeWord(".bb/runtime/config/greeter-agent/consul.json"), includes:
	}
	@docCode("consul.json (excerpt)", consulSidecarJSON)
	@docList(
		"retry_join points the agent at the Consul server; it keeps retrying until the join succeeds.",
		"The service block is the registration. The agent owns it and keeps the server's catalog in sync with it.",
		"The default check opens a TCP connection to the greeter and completes a TLS handshake, verified against the lab CA for greeter.bb.internal. A full handshake means the greeter logs no aborted connections.",
		"With register.check set, the check is an HTTPS GET to that path instead.",
		"client_addr 127.0.0.1 keeps the agent's own HTTPS API on loopback, inside the greeter's namespace.",
	)
	@docH2("lifecycle", "Joining and leaving")
	@docP() {
		What actually happens on @docCodeWord("bb up"): the agent starts once the greeter has started and the Consul server is healthy. It joins the server over encrypted gossip, connects to it over mutually authenticated TLS RPC, and syncs its service and check into the catalog. The agent runs the check itself, every 5 seconds, and reports the result to the server. Its own Docker health check passes once @docCodeWord("consul catalog services") lists @docCodeWord("greeter")&#46;
	}
	@docP() {
		@docCodeWord("leave_on_terminate") is on, so when the agent receives SIGTERM it sends a Leave message to the cluster. When a node leaves gracefully, Consul deregisters every service on it at once. Stop the agent and the greeter keeps running, but no one can find it.
	}
	@docH2("self", "Alternative: self-registration")
	@docP() {
		The consul/self-registration starter, the labeled alternative, does the same job in application code. With no local agent, @docCodeWord("Agent().ServiceRegister") goes to the agent at @docCodeWord("CONSUL_HTTP_ADDR"), which is the server.
	}
	@docCode("cmd/self-greeter/main.go", consulSelfRegisterCode)
	@docP() {
		This sends @docCodeWord("PUT /v1/agent/service/register") with the registration as JSON. The server agent now owns the entry and runs its check. Cleaning up becomes the program's job too:
	}
	@docCode("cmd/self-greeter/main.go", consulSelfDeregisterCode)
	@docList(
		"On SIGTERM or Ctrl-C, the service deregisters itself with PUT /v1/agent/service/deregister/<id>. The starter's Dockerfile runs the binary as the container's main process, so it receives the signal.",
		"If the process is killed instead, nothing deregisters it. Its check turns critical and lookups skip it, but the entry stays.",
		"DeregisterCriticalServiceAfter covers that case: once the check has been critical for a minute, Consul removes the service.",
		"Every service that registers itself repeats this code, in every language it is written in. That is why the sidecar is recommended.",
	)
	@docTerminal("bb starter consul/self-registration", "bb up", "bb logs self-caller -f")
}

templ consulHealthChecks() {
	@docLead() {
		A health check is a probe Consul runs, or a status your service reports, that decides whether an instance is returned to callers.
	}
	@docH2("states", "Passing, warning, critical")
	@docP() {
		Every check is in one of three states. A new check starts as critical unless its registration sets an initial status, so a just-registered instance is not returned until its first check passes.
	}
	@docList(
		"passing: the probe succeeded.",
		"warning: degraded. For an HTTP check, a 429 Too Many Requests response.",
		"critical: the probe failed, timed out, or a TTL ran out.",
	)
	@docH2("kinds", "Kinds of check")
	@docList(
		"TCP: Consul connects to host:port. A connection that opens is passing; a refused or timed-out one is critical.",
		"TCP with TLS (tcp_use_tls): after connecting, Consul completes a TLS handshake and by default expects a valid certificate. tls_server_name sets the name to verify. This is the default check of Breadboard's sidecar.",
		"HTTP or HTTPS: Consul sends a GET to the URL. Any 2xx is passing, 429 is warning, anything else is critical.",
		"TTL: Consul runs nothing. The service must report its own status before the TTL expires.",
		"Others exist: script, gRPC, HTTP/2 ping, UDP, Docker, and alias checks.",
	)
	@docP() {
		Probing checks are run by the agent that holds the registration, on their @docCodeWord("interval"), each bounded by @docCodeWord("timeout")&#46; In Breadboard, both kinds of agent have @docCodeWord("enable_agent_tls_for_checks") on, so TLS and HTTPS checks verify the service's certificate against the lab CA. To switch the sidecar to an HTTPS check, set a path:
	}
	@docCode("experiment.yaml", consulHTTPCheckYAML)
	@docH2("ttl", "TTL checks")
	@docP() {
		A TTL check inverts the direction: the service says it is healthy. Useful when only the process knows, for example a worker that should report only while its queue is moving.
	}
	@docCode("cmd/worker/main.go", consulTTLCode)
	@docP() {
		@docCodeWord("UpdateTTL") sets the check's status and resets its TTL clock. If no update arrives within 15 seconds, Consul marks the check critical. A hung process stops updating, so it drops out of lookups.
	}
	@docH2("lookups", "How a failing check changes lookups")
	@docP() {
		Nothing is pushed to callers. Each lookup with @docCodeWord("passing") set returns only instances whose checks are all passing, so a failing instance simply stops appearing in the next answer. A warning check also excludes it from a passing-only lookup. Without @docCodeWord("passing"), every registered instance is returned with its checks, and filtering is the caller's job.
	}
	@docSteps(
		"Apply consul/service-discovery and run bb up.",
		"Run docker stop bb-consul-lab-greeter-1.",
		"Within one check interval the check turns critical, and the caller logs no healthy greeter instances.",
		"Start the greeter again and the next passing check brings it back.",
	)
	@docH2("deregister", "Removing dead registrations")
	@docP() {
		A failing check hides an instance; it does not remove it. @docCodeWord("DeregisterCriticalServiceAfter") on a service's check tells Consul to deregister the service, with all its checks, once the check has been critical for that long. The minimum is one minute, and the process that reaps critical services runs every 30 seconds, so removal can take a little longer than the setting. The self-registration starter sets it to @docCodeWord("1m"); the sidecar does not need it, because the agent deregisters the app when it leaves.
	}
}

templ consulKV() {
	@docLead() {
		Consul's servers also store keys and values, replicated across every server. It is a small, basic store for configuration and coordination, not a database.
	}
	@docH2("what", "What it is for")
	@docList(
		"Configuration values shared by several services, such as a greeting or a timeout.",
		"Feature flags that change at runtime without a redeploy.",
		"Locks and leader election, built on Consul sessions.",
	)
	@docP() {
		Keys are slash-separated paths by convention, such as @docCodeWord("config/greeter/greeting")&#46; Each value is at most 512 KB.
	}
	@docH2("put-get", "Writing and reading a key")
	@docCode("main.go", consulKVCode)
	@docP() {
		@docCodeWord("Put") sends @docCodeWord("PUT /v1/kv/config/greeter/greeting") with the value as the body. @docCodeWord("Get") returns a @docCodeWord("*KVPair"), or nil with no error when the key does not exist, so check for nil before using it. A pair also carries a @docCodeWord("ModifyIndex"), which changes on every write to that key.
	}
	@docH2("blocking", "Waiting for a change")
	@docP() {
		Instead of polling, a reader can block until a key changes. Pass the last index you saw as @docCodeWord("WaitIndex"):
	}
	@docCode("main.go", consulBlockingCode)
	@docP() {
		What actually happens: the request carries @docCodeWord("?index=N") and the server holds it open until the data's index moves past N or the wait time runs out (5 minutes by default, 10 at most). A return is not a guarantee of change, so compare @docCodeWord("LastIndex") with the index you sent before acting. The same mechanism works on health queries, which is how a caller can keep a service's instance list current without asking on every request.
	}
}

templ consulInBreadboard() {
	@docLead() {
		What Breadboard runs when you add Consul, and how it is wired.
	}
	@docH2("server", "One server")
	@docP() {
		The service runs the official @docCodeWord("hashicorp/consul") image as a single server in the datacenter @docCodeWord("bb"), with @docCodeWord("bootstrap_expect: 1") so it elects itself leader. Its Raft data lives on a volume and survives restarts. With one server there is no fault tolerance: if it stops, lookups and KV reads fail, even though the services themselves are fine. The container's health check runs @docCodeWord("consul operator raft list-peers") over HTTPS, which succeeds only once a leader exists.
	}
	@docH2("listeners", "HTTPS on 8501, little else")
	@docP() {
		Apps reach the HTTP API at @docCodeWord("https://consul.bb.internal:8501"), with a certificate issued by your Breadboard CA. The plain HTTP port, plaintext gRPC, DNS and WAN gossip are disabled in the server's configuration. A plain HTTP listener on 8500 exists only when an app asks for one with @docCodeWord("tls.mode: off"), bound to the private network shared with that app. The web UI is enabled on the HTTPS port.
	}
	@docH2("security", "Gossip and RPC encryption")
	@docList(
		"LAN gossip stays on so sidecar agents can join. It is encrypted with a key Breadboard generates (32 random bytes, stored in .bb/secrets.json) and passes to each agent with -encrypt on the command line, never in a config file.",
		"Agents verify incoming and outgoing gossip, so an agent without the key cannot join.",
		"Server RPC requires TLS with a client certificate from the lab CA. Sidecar agents present their app's certificate.",
		"ACLs are out of scope: any client that can reach the API can read and write.",
	)
	@docH2("env", "What your app receives")
	@docList(
		"CONSUL_HTTP_ADDR: https://consul.bb.internal:8501.",
		"CONSUL_CACERT: the trust bundle, so the client verifies the server.",
		"BB_FQDN and BB_SERVICE: the app's own address and name, used by the self-registration starter.",
	)
	@docH2("explore", "The Catalog tab")
	@docP() {
		The consul service's page in the dashboard has a Catalog tab. It lists every service in the catalog with its instance count, health (passing, warning or critical) and tags, and every agent (node) with its address. A sidecar appears there as a node named after its app. The tab reads @docCodeWord("/v1/catalog/services"), @docCodeWord("/v1/health/state/any") and @docCodeWord("/v1/catalog/nodes") from inside the Consul container. To use the CLI yourself:
	}
	@docTerminal("docker exec -it bb-consul-lab-greeter-agent-1 consul catalog services", "docker exec -it bb-consul-lab-greeter-agent-1 consul members")
}
